How does it interact with not-normally-wishable-for items?  Does the 
"already exists" or the "no-wishing" check get made first?  Be careful 
not to unbalance the quest, though it would be nice to be able to steal 
stuff back from covetous monsters.

>   Critiques and ideas for expansion welcome. Some I've already thought
> of, with comments:
> 
>   * Wishing for items that are currently on other levels (e.g., "my box
> named Stash"). I'd like to do this one, but it would be a real pain,
> because levels other than the current one are stored on disk.
> 
>   * Wishing for named items without a monster "owner" (e.g., "Magicbane"
> when it's just been stolen by a nymph). Also a good idea, and doable,
> but I think it would need some more infrastructure.
> 
>   * An even better way out of that jam: wish for "the dwarf's corpse"!
> Combine with previous ideas to get Fluffy's corpse for reanimation
> (whether Fluffy is dead or not, I suppose!).
> 
>   * Wishing for things on the ground; but how do you specify them?
> "That cockatrice corpse"?

This brings to mind a generalisation possibility:
"Wish for an item by typing the name? [ynq]"
If you say yes, then you get the normal wishing prompt, if quit, then 
you don't wish for anything, and if no, then you go to the second prompt:
"Wish for an item by locating it? [ynq]"
If you say no, then you go back to the first prompt, if quit, then you 
don't wish for anything, but if you say yes:
"Where is the item?"
You use hklyubn<> to navigate and . to select.
Then:
"Which item?" + inventory list, or "Which item (type the name)?"

There would need to be a way to differentiate between a container and 
its contents.  Also, pressing escape should mostly allow you to back out 
of one question rather than cancelling the whole wishing process, in 
case you forget which bag contains something.

